## Why This Is Asked
Assess hands-on orchestration across Azure components during incidents, focusing on tenant isolation, automated rollback safety, and auditable controls.

## Key Concepts
- UEBA-driven automation (Defender for Cloud/Sentinel)
- Per-tenant network isolation (NSG, Private Endpoint)
- Key Vault key rotation and re-encryption
- Token revocation and Conditional Access control
- Canary-based rollback mechanisms
- Centralized auditing and alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator snippet
async function quarantineTenant(tenantId){
  await applyPerTenantNSG(tenantId);
  await rotateKeyVaultKeys(tenantId);
  await revokeTokensAndDisableCA(tenantId);
  await rollbackToLastGoodRelease(tenantId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical guardrails for real-world, globally distributed Azure workloads, focusing on logs/telemetry leakage, secret rotation, and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Managed Identities with least privilege RBAC
- Key Vault references over inline secrets
- Azure Policy gates (no secrets in env vars/connection strings)
- Application Insights masking and diagnostic redaction
- Canary deployments with telemetry-driven rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js example: fetch secret via DefaultAzureCredential and KeyVaultSecretClient
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require('@azure/identity');
const { SecretClient } = require('@azure/keyvault-secrets');
const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const client = new SecretClient("https://<kv-name>.vault.azure.net/", credential);
async function getSecret(name) {
  const secret = await client.getSecret(name);
  return secret.value;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the guardrail during a canary rollout?
- What logging changes would you implement to ensure no secrets are emitted even in error traces?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-region data governance with fine-grained access control, dynamic masking, and comprehensive auditability using Azure Purview, Synapse RBAC, Conditional Access, and Privileged Identity Management.

## Key Concepts
- Data classification and lineage tracking with Azure Purview
- Fine-grained access control in Synapse and ADLS Gen2
- Dynamic data masking with query-time enforcement
- Privileged Identity Management and Conditional Access integration
- End-to-end rollout, rollback, and testing methodologies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: determine if masking is required for a query
function requiresMasking(userId, dataset, column) {
  // Check Purview classification and user permissions
  const classification = purview.getClassification(dataset, column);
  const userAccess = synapseRBAC.checkPermissions(userId, dataset);

  // Apply masking based on sensitivity and user role
  return classification.sensitivity === 'High' && 
         !userAccess.hasColumnLevelAccess;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to implement zero-trust service-to-service authentication in a multi-tenant Azure environment.

## Key Concepts
- Zero-trust service-to-service authentication using workload identities and OAuth/OIDC tokens
- Kubernetes mTLS via service mesh or sidecar proxies
- Per-tenant Azure Key Vault instances with automated key rotation
- Infrastructure-as-Code policy gates to enforce security baselines (no secrets in configuration, template signing, compliance validation)
- Comprehensive audit trails and User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) for detecting credential misuse and anomalous access patterns
- Incident response planning with clear rollback procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: gateway token validation
function isTokenValid(token, tenantId) {
  const claims = parseToken(token);
  return claims.tenantId === tenantId && 
         claims.iss === 'https://sts.windows.net/' + tenantId &&
         claims.exp > Date.now() / 1000;
}

function validateRequest(req, tenantId) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token || !isTokenValid(token, tenantId)) {
    return { valid: false, reason: 'Invalid or missing token' };
  }
  return { valid: true, claims: parseToken(token) };
}
```
